# .env.example — the TRACKED template for this project's environment file.
# KIT-CLASS: MIXED — the kit's entries below are complete; the project block is a fill-in.
#
# THE NEVER-COMMIT POLICY, stated once:
#   `.env` is gitignored and MUST NEVER be committed. This `.env.example` is the only
#   tracked copy, and it holds NAMES and comments — never a real value, not even a
#   short-lived one, and not even commented out. A secret that ever reached a commit is
#   compromised for the life of the repository; rotate it rather than rewriting history.
#   See PROJECT.md § Credential doctrine.
#
#   cp .env.example .env      # then edit .env
#
# `./setup.sh` seeds .env from this file on first run and never overwrites an existing one.

# ── The kit's own entries ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Outbound notifications. OPTIONAL, and OFF by default: with no backend configured the
# notification script is a deliberate silent no-op, so nothing in the process depends on
# it being reachable. Uncomment and name a backend only if you want notifications.
# The contract is process/contracts/notification.md; whether it is on here is recorded in
# CLAUDE.md § "What is ON and what is OFF here".
# NOTIFY_BACKEND=<your backend>
#
# The rest of the notification path's names, so this section is what its header says
# it is. Each is OPTIONAL and each has a default the script applies when the name is
# absent — setting one here only matters when you want a different answer.
#
# Which event classes are sent, and how loudly. Comma-separated class lists.
# Defaults: attention,blocked,done buzz the phone; milestone is sent silently.
# NOTIFY_WITH_MSG_AND_ALERT=<classes that alert>
# NOTIFY_WITH_MSG_ONLY=<classes that arrive silently>
#
# What a FAILED backend self-test does at setup time. Default: continue.
# NOTIFY_ON_SETUP_FAILURE=<continue|abort>   # DECLARATIVE: nothing in the kit branches on it.
#   `notify.sh test` exits non-zero on a failed delivery; this states what your project wants done
#   about that, and your own session-start wiring (or the agent reading the warning) is what acts.
#
# The name notifications are labelled with. Default: derived from the repository.
# NOTIFY_PROJECT=<name>
#
# The telegram backend's own names, needed only when that backend is the one named
# above. NAMES ONLY — the token is a CREDENTIAL and the never-commit policy at the
# top of this file governs it: no value here, not even a short-lived one, not even
# commented out.
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID=

# ── <your project credentials> ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# FILL ME. One block per credential, and each block carries FOUR things:
#   1. the variable NAME (no value);
#   2. what it is for, in one line;
#   3. whether it may MUTATE anything — the read/write separation is the whole point of
#      listing them separately, so a read-only task can never be handed a writing key;
#   4. what fails, and how loudly, when it is absent.
#
# Do NOT let this file become the authoritative list of required permissions or scopes:
# that list belongs wherever your code declares it, and this file is a convenience copy
# for setting an account up. Two authoring sites for one requirement is how the two start
# disagreeing.
#
# <e.g. READ_TOKEN=            # read-only; may mutate NOTHING. Absent → every read fails loudly.>
# <e.g. WRITE_TOKEN=           # may mutate. Used ONLY against the disposable target
#                              #   (process/doctrine/live-resources.md). Absent → write tests skip.>
# <e.g. TARGET_ENDPOINT=       # the base address the client talks to. No default; never a
#                              #   production address in this file.>

# ── The publication remote ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# KWT_REMOTE is the ONE name every kit operation honours: the board mover, the archive
# sweep, the subtask mover, the PR landing, the drift report, the initializer, and the
# release push. It is a remote NAME, never a URL — the worktree machinery resolves the
# trunk through refs/remotes/$KWT_REMOTE/HEAD, and a URL there fails that silently.
# Working on a fork? Set this one thing and everything follows it.
#KWT_REMOTE=origin
#
# RELEASE_REMOTE overrides the above for the RELEASE PUSH ONLY, and is the one place a
# URL is accepted. Leave it unset unless your releases go somewhere your board does not.
#RELEASE_REMOTE=
